## Issues

### Active (alignment pass — post-move drift)

- [ ] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` frontmatter
  `scope: src/tools/init/provisionBrain`
  Stale path — module moved to `src/service/install/provisionBrain/`.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.desired[4] (folder tree as architecture).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired[6]:
  "Every InitStep returned is a planning artifact only — no files are
  written to disk by this helper. The calling agent or applySteps
  orchestrator handles all writes."
  "The calling agent" framing assumes the MCP tool's agent-driven shape.
  The CLI consumer is not an agent and does not interpret the planning
  artifacts the same way. The planner-only contract is correct at the
  service level; the consumer-shape phrasing should move to a separate
  outcome or be rewritten consumer-neutrally.
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` (misaligned — install
  service identity drift); ancestor `src/.aide` -> outcomes.desired[3]
  (client-specific logic confined to aide_init).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Context: "the init
  orchestrator's apply layer handles actual writes"
  Same identity drift as above — "init orchestrator" hardcodes the MCP
  tool consumer when the apply layer is now part of the install service
  consumed by both consumers.
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` identity drift.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

### Resolved (prior QA cycle — kept for retro context)

- [x] Seed-semantic idempotency outcomes verified clean — no drift
  detected between spec and code in the seed-vs-canonical-template
  branch. Two-idempotency-mode distinction codified explicitly in the
  Strategy section.

## Retro

Two layers of drift in this spec:

1. **Architectural-shift drift (current):** Folder rename and new
   consumer (CLI) should have triggered a `/aide:spec` pass. The
   "init orchestrator" / "calling agent" terminology hardcodes the
   MCP-tool consumer.

2. **Prior cycle (resolved):** The seed-semantic / canonical-template
   idempotency distinction was a deliberate design pivot that landed
   cleanly. The spec's Strategy section codified the two-idempotency-mode
   distinction explicitly and named the distinguishing question ("who
   owns the bytes after first write?"). The parameterized regression
   test (`describe.each` over four on-disk states) is the right
   regression anchor for a data-loss-class invariant.

Stage to watch going forward: the prior cycle introduced placeholder
wikilinks (`[[your-conventions-note]]`, `[[your-folder-structure-note]]`)
and a negative assertion in the test. Any future edit to
`PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` that adds named wikilinks (even well-intentioned
ones) will trip the negative assertion. The spec's
`outcomes.undesired[1]` is the first place a future implementor should
look when that assertion fails.
